      <h1>Stop Chasing Invoices: How Freelancers Can Track Client Payment Promises</h1>
      <time datetime="2026-03-23">March 23, 2026</time>
    </header>

    <p>The client said "we'll pay on Net 30" in a Slack message. It's now day 47. You've sent two follow-up emails. They've gone silent.</p>

    <p>Sound familiar? If you're one of the 59 million freelancers in the US, you've been here. The promise was clear. The evidence exists — somewhere in your message history. But digging through months of Slack threads, emails, and text conversations to compile a timeline? That's a full afternoon you don't have.</p>

    <h2>The Freelancer's Documentation Problem</h2>

    <p>Freelancers operate on informal agreements more than any other professional group. Scope changes discussed over text. Payment timelines confirmed in a DM. Verbal commitments followed up with a "just to confirm" email that nobody saves properly.</p>

    <p>When things go well, the informality is a feature — it's fast, it's flexible, it's human. When things go wrong, the informality is the weapon used against you. "I never said Net 30." "We agreed the scope included revisions." "That quote was an estimate, not a commitment."</p>

    <h2>What to Document (and When)</h2>

    <p>The commitments that matter most for freelancers fall into predictable categories:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Payment timelines:</strong> "We'll pay within 30 days of delivery." Screenshot the moment it's said.</li>
      <li><strong>Scope boundaries:</strong> "This project includes 3 rounds of revisions." Screenshot it before work begins.</li>
      <li><strong>Rate confirmations:</strong> "Your rate is $150/hour for this engagement." Screenshot it.</li>
      <li><strong>Delivery expectations:</strong> "We need the first draft by the 15th." Screenshot it — this protects both sides.</li>
      <li><strong>Expense approvals:</strong> "Go ahead and book the $500 software license, we'll reimburse." Screenshot it before you spend a dime.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>From Screenshot to Leverage</h2>

    <p>A screenshot in your camera roll is potential evidence. A screenshot in a tracked, timestamped ledger with deadline alerts is leverage.</p>

    <p>The difference is organization. When you can pull up a chronological timeline showing: "Client agreed to Net 30 on January 5. Invoice delivered January 8. First follow-up sent February 8. Second follow-up sent February 15. Day 47: still unpaid" — that's not just a complaint. That's a case.</p>

    <h2>How Seddly Helps</h2>

    <p>Seddly automates the documentation that freelancers do manually today. Screenshot the promise, and Seddly extracts: who said it, what they committed to, and when it's due. When the deadline passes, you get a notification. When it's time to escalate, you generate a timeline.</p>

    <p>No spreadsheets. No folder systems. No "I'll organize this later" that never happens.</p>

